    "done_when": "The velocity view is reachable in the plan SPA as a \"Velocity\" work tab beside Sprints, Graph and Crew.\n\nreckon/serve.py serves GET /crew/velocity?project=NAME&since=ISO&until=ISO&fields=...&limit=N&cursor=C. It calls the same function in reckon/velocity.py that crew(view=\"velocity\") and `reckon crew velocity` call, so all three surfaces return one payload. It resolves the project checkout through the server's existing mounts. It refuses a missing since with a 400 carrying the same refusal text. Its default window, when the caller gives none, is the last 14 days, set in serve.py and named in the response.\n\nA new docs/ui/velocity.jsx renders that payload and derives nothing:\n- the per-project table: promotions split into implementation and review, lines landed by class, the 7-day deletion share, dispatch-to-completion median and p75, attempts per landed node, review share;\n- the per-lane and per-day tables;\n- the weekly plans opened and closed;\n- a window picker that re-requests with since and until.\nWhile a request is in flight it shows a loading state that names the window, because a cold window can take minutes. It is registered in the SPA's component load list before shell.jsx, the way crew.jsx is; docs/evidence/inflight-visibility-landed.html:288-307 records why that order matters. The tab is added to WORK_TABS in docs/ui/shell-route.jsx.\n\ntests/test_serve_velocity_route.py starts the server's handler on a synthesised mount under tmp_path, with repositories and a ledger built the way tests/test_crew_velocity_view.py builds them. It asserts:\n- the route's JSON equals crew(view=\"velocity\") for the same window, a parity case;\n- a missing since returns 400;\n- the SPA load list names velocity.jsx before shell.jsx;\n- WORK_TABS carries the velocity key.\nNothing is read or written outside tmp_path.\n\nThat file and the existing crew-route and serve tests keep their base failing ids and add none. The manifest writes baseline_suite and after_suite each as one line of armed JSON, and every gate log opens with one line naming its revision, tree and command and ends with EXIT=. The declared negative control is applied and observed to fail, and its log's absolute path is in the manifest with an EXIT= line and the FAILED id.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Put the velocity view in the plan SPA as a Velocity tab, rendering a server route that returns the same payload as the MCP view and the CLI",
